points.explanation:
This {そう} gives your impression from what you can **see right now**, before you know for sure. 'It looks ~.'

- {い}-adjectives: drop {い}: {おいしい} → {おいしそう} 'looks tasty', {高[たか]い} → {高[たか]そう}
- {な}-adjectives: stem + {そう}: {元気[げんき]そう} 'looks well', {丈夫[じょうぶ]そう}
- Verbs: {ます}-stem + {そう} means 'looks like it's about to ~': {雨[あめ]が降[ふ]りそうだ} 'It looks like it's going to rain', {落[お]ちそう} 'looks like it'll fall'
- Irregular: {いい} → {よさそう}, {ない} → {なさそう}; negatives: {おいしくなさそう}

{そう} works like a {な}-adjective: {おいしそうなケーキ} 'a delicious-looking cake', {楽[たの]しそうに遊[あそ]ぶ} 'play happily (looking like they're having fun)'.

It's not used for things obvious at a glance, like colours: say {赤[あか]い}, not {赤[あか]そう}.

This second {そうだ} reports information you got from somewhere else: a person, the news, a forecast. 'I hear that ~ / Apparently ~.' {明日[あした]は雪[ゆき]が降[ふ]るそうです} 'I hear it's going to snow tomorrow.'

The big difference from 'looks like' {そう} is the connection: hearsay {そうだ} attaches to the **full plain form**, including {だ} and past tense:

- {降[ふ]るそうだ}, {降[ふ]ったそうだ}, {降[ふ]らないそうだ}
- {おいしいそうだ}, {静[しず]かだそうだ}, {学生[がくせい]だそうだ}

So {おいしそう} = 'looks tasty' but {おいしいそうだ} = 'I hear it's tasty'.

Name the source with {〜によると} or {〜の話[はなし]では}. Hearsay {そうだ} itself doesn't go into the past or negative; change what's inside instead.
